Background
The purpose of this assessment was to evaluate the security posture of NovaTech’s network architecture and identify vulnerabilities that could be exploited by attackers and proffer security recommendations to mitigate these risks.
Objectives
This report seeks to Identify the gaps and vulnerabilities within NovaTech systems  Assess risks associated to these vulnerabilities. Provide actionable remediation recommendations Evaluate possible attack pathways.
Scope of Assessment
NovaTech operates a flat network (192.168.10.0/24) with five main devices. As a result, the assessment covers the following assets:
•	Web Server (192.168.10.20): this shows repeated connection with an external ip address. 
•	File Server (192.168.10.25) responsible for storing company documents
•	Database Server (192.168.10.30) contains sensitive documents
•	Employee Workstations (192.168.10.15)
•	Internal Network (192.168.10.0/24)
Environment/Network Analysis
Network	192.168.10.0/24
Accessibility	Internal & Internet-facing (Webserver)
Services	HTTP, FTP, SMB, MYSQL
Authentication	Username/Password

Key Findings
Analysis of network traffic revealed several important observations:
•	The Web Server (192.168.10.20) shows repeated outbound HTTP connections to external IP addresses, indicating suspicious behavior.
•	Multiple protocols are in use, including: HTTP (web traffic), SMB (file sharing), MySQL (database communication), FTP (file transfer), DNS (name resolution).
•	The File Server (192.168.10.25) is responsible for storing company documents.
•	The Web Server communicates directly with the Database Server (192.168.10.30), creating a potential attack path.
The web server is likely compromised or communicating with an external attacker.
Tools Used
Nmap, Hydra (Brute Force testing), FFUF (Directory Enumeration), Siem Log Analysis, Manual Testing Techniques. 
